sunrise in kiev once again the sound of  gunfire in the distance as russian  forces come ever closer to ukraine's  capital  southwest of the city plumes of smoke  rise above an oil depot in vasselkeev  ukrainian forces said the fire began  after a russian airstrike  some western intelligence officials say  moscow's invasion is slower than  expected but the pace of russia's true  movement means little to people in  places like the southern city of volnova  look we were sitting in basements and  they were walking around everywhere  shooting we couldn't leave the basement  for over a day then i walked up to a  soldier and i said boys my mum is  bedridden up there she's a grandma and i  can't go up there the guys wanted to  help me bring her down into the basement  but when we went up there she had  already died it was already too late  in her kiev emergency workers search for  survivors in what's left of an apartment  block the eastern city has been shelled  for days by russian forces as has  chernahiv in the north of ukraine  reminders the past are everywhere in  this city but the present is  relentlessly bleak  the enemy is continuing air and rocket  shelling of the city of cherny heave  the peaceful population is dying and  there are many injured the enemy is  hitting civilian infrastructure western  ukraine has been spared the worst of the  conflict for now but things could be  changing in lutsk a russian missile  attack destroyed an airfield on the  city's outskirts  four soldiers were killed in the attack  on the airfield the funerals of two of  them have just taken place in this  church in the center of the city the  mayor of lutsk says the people here  though won't be intimidated by russian  forces  people just a little scared  but  we have no totally panic in our city we  we don't see  people live in our city we don't see  refugees live in our city and we  are still  we are still  believe  that ukraine  will win  110 kilometers from the border with  russia's ally belarus  some ukrainian officials say belarusian  troops could join the war others say  that's unlikely  if they want to come here let them come  but we will destroy them  and send them back in body bags  i hope belarus would reconsider their  position there's enough war it's a  massacre  the shelter in the city is now home for  vyaches slav and his family he says they  fled more than a week of unbearable  russian shelling his children cried  every day  ludsk is close to belarus but it's also  not far from the polish border  vyacheslav says they could go to germany  but for now he just feels lost  they took everything from me  i had a comfortable house the kids all  had their own room  now where will i go  and for what i'll have to start from  scratch  i'm not 20 anymore i'm 41.  for people caught up in this war there  are painful decisions often there are no  choices parents across ukraine are  struggling trapped in a terrible  situation they didn't create holding  their children's futures in their hands  natasha butler al jazeera lutzc  northwest ukraine
 

